WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.294
[Music starts] [Audio Description - not captioned]

00:00:04.294 --> 00:00:10.147
[Teresa Przytycka, PhD] My research focuses&nbsp;
on building computational methods to address&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.147 --> 00:00:14.543
problems in in biomedical sciences.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.107
We are particularly interested in&nbsp;
developing new methods to study diseases,

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.240
with particular interest actually in cancer.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.560
Before, people have been looking at diseases&nbsp;
from the perspective - okay, here's a gene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.960
something wrong happened to this&nbsp;
gene, and you have a disease.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.358
But complex diseases does not work this way.

00:00:41.629 --> 00:00:45.120
The usual is something happened to this&nbsp;
pathway, something happened to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.806
process. And when something happened&nbsp;
to this process, you have a disease.

00:00:53.878 --> 00:00:59.040
It's not so easy to identify the&nbsp;
pathway, when you look gene-by-genes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.240
because it may be that every&nbsp;
individual perturbation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.240
when you look from gene-by-genes perspective,&nbsp;
is very small and it's hard to capture.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.920
But when you understand that you are to&nbsp;
look at the pathway in group of genes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.160
the signals gets amplified, and&nbsp;
you may be able to identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:20.240
processes that you wouldn't see if&nbsp;
you would look at the genes in isolation.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:26.343
So we are trying to look at the diseases from&nbsp;
the perspective of this regulated pathways.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:41.120
Some of our recent study in cancer research are&nbsp;
related to understanding of the relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.753
between mutations patients may&nbsp;
have and say drug response.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.560
Assume that we identify a&nbsp;
pathway, we can ask the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.320
"does the patient has a mutation in&nbsp;
this pathway?", and this gives us some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.480
way of predicting whether or not&nbsp;
individual will responds to drug.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:08.143
Those questions are indeed very much&nbsp;
driven by personalized medicine.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.760
I think that the goal would be some of the methods&nbsp;
that I've developed will have a really lasting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:24.000
effect and also that some of the biological&nbsp;
findings would be important for human health.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.511
[Music and audio description continues without captions]

00:02:37.129 --> 00:02:40.000
[Music continues]

00:02:50.091 --> 00:02:51.557
[Music fades to silence]

